From: PowerVLC
Subject: [PATCH] Harden repeated BD-J session shutdown

libbluray keeps its JVM bridge and action manager in process-wide state.  A
player can nevertheless close one input on a worker while another input is
being opened.  PowerVLC serializes those complete lifetimes at the access
module boundary; make the bridge defensive too:

* fail cleanly when attaching a native thread to the existing JVM fails,
  instead of dereferencing an invalid JNIEnv or trying to create a second VM;
* initialize and clear BDJActionManager's running state correctly;
* retain and join the watchdog thread during shutdown.

The latter two changes prevent one action-queue/monitor pair from being leaked
for every disc opened during a long player session.

Recent JDKs removed ThreadGroup.stop(), and Thread.stop() throws
UnsupportedOperationException since Java 20.  Fall back to enumerating the
Xlet group for older runtimes that can still stop individual threads, but
never wait without a deadline for a non-cooperative disc IXC call.  Otherwise
bdj_close() waits forever when the user quits during playback.

